  8059                                  ; cmd_alias_invoke: called from dispatch when cmd_buf matched an alias
  8060                                  ; name, with [alias_match_idx] set to that alias's table index. Runs
  8061                                  ; the alias's stored body exactly like a freshly-typed line, through
  8062                                  ; the same chain processor (process_chain or process_chain_rr) that's
  8063                                  ; currently active, so ; chaining and ~ piping inside the alias work
  8064                                  ; normally and nested alias calls work too.
  8065                                  ;
  8066                                  ; This has to protect two pieces of global, shared state that would
  8067                                  ; otherwise be clobbered by that nested call:
  8068                                  ;   - chain_scan_buf: the outer process_chain(_rr) is still mid-scan
  8069                                  ;     over it (via r12/r13), so it's backed up on the stack, restored
  8070                                  ;     after the nested call returns. r12/r13 are ordinary registers,
  8071                                  ;     saved/restored by normal callee-saved discipline, so only the
  8072                                  ;     memory needs protecting, not the pointers themselves.
