 12448                                  ; nic_send_raw: rcx = byte length of the frame already built in nic_tx_buf.
 12449                                  ; Loads TSAD0 with the buffer address, writes the length to TSD0 to trigger
 12450                                  ; the DMA+transmit, then waits for TxStatOk (bit15) in TSD0. Returns CF=0 on
 12451                                  ; success. Callers always wait for completion, so no in-flight TX precedes us.
 12452                                  ;
 12453                                  ; Pad undersized frames up to the Ethernet minimum (60 bytes, before the
 12454                                  ; 4-byte FCS the NIC appends) before handing off to any backend. QEMU's
 12455                                  ; device models are lenient about short frames, but real hardware is not
 12456                                  ; guaranteed to be - this is exactly what was going on with the RTL8168
 12457                                  ; "TX error" on ARP broadcasts (42 bytes): no PCI bus error, no stuck NPQ,
 12458                                  ; the frame eventually completes, just unreliably/late relative to our
 12459                                  ; completion-wait budget. DHCP frames never showed this because they're
 12460                                  ; already well over 60 bytes. Doing this once, here, covers every backend
 12461                                  ; (rtl8139/e1000/rtl8168) instead of patching each short-frame caller.
