 15947                                  %include "zip.asm"
     1                              <1> ; ============================================================
     2                              <1> ;  ZIP.ASM  --  stored-mode (uncompressed) .zip pack / unpack
     3                              <1> ; ============================================================
     4                              <1> ; Scope decision (locked in up front, per this project's own
     5                              <1> ; convention of deciding before coding): this implements the ZIP
     6                              <1> ; container FORMAT only, using compression method 0 ("stored" - raw
     7                              <1> ; bytes, no compression math). Real DEFLATE compression/decompression
     8                              <1> ; is NOT implemented; it's a much larger, separate undertaking. The
     9                              <1> ; files this produces are still ordinary, valid .zip files - any
    10                              <1> ; unzip tool on any OS opens them fine, since "stored" is a normal,
    11                              <1> ; standard part of the format, not a proprietary shortcut. unpack
    12                              <1> ; extracts foreign archives too, as long as every entry in them also
    13                              <1> ; uses method 0; a DEFLATE'd entry is a clean, explicit refusal
    14                              <1> ; rather than garbage output.
    15                              <1> ;
    16                              <1> ; Commands added (kernel.asm wires these into dispatch + help text):
    17                              <1> ;   pack <folder>      writes <folder>.zip into the current directory
    18                              <1> ;   unpack <file.zip>   extracts into the current directory
    19                              <1> ;
    20                              <1> ; Depends on kernel.asm's fs_* API (fs_resolve_path, fs_find_child,
    21                              <1> ; fs_create_node, fs_read_binary_file, fs_write_binary_file,
    22                              <1> ; fs_file_len), the node_type/node_name/node_parent tables, and the
    23                              <1> ; shared str_len/str_copy/str_append/str_eq/int_to_str/print_string*
    24                              <1> ; helpers - the same primitives cmd_cpy/cmd_mkfl/take/give already
    25                              <1> ; use. Reuses fs_io_buf (EDIT_MAX bytes) as a one-file-at-a-time
    26                              <1> ; staging buffer while packing, the same way cmd_cat/edit do.
    27                              <1> ;
    28                              <1> ; A whole packed archive - or a whole archive being unpacked - is
    29                              <1> ; staged in zip_buf, a single EDIT_MAX (20480-byte) buffer, mirroring
    30                              <1> ; the same per-file size cap every other file operation in this OS
    31                              <1> ; already has (mkfl/edit/cat/take/give). A folder that packs to more
    32                              <1> ; than that, or a .zip bigger than that, is a clean "too big" error,
    33                              <1> ; not a silent truncation. ZIP_MAX_ENTRIES (64) is a similar soft
    34                              <1> ; cap on file COUNT per archive, mostly there so the fixed
    35                              <1> ; central-directory-descriptor table has a size at all.
    36                              <1> ;
    37                              <1> ; Known gaps / deferred (see phases.txt / CHANGELOG for the writeup):
    38                              <1> ;   - No compression (see scope decision above).
    39                              <1> ;   - No empty-folder entries: pack only emits entries for files: an
    40                              <1> ;     empty subfolder isn't represented in the archive at all (a
    41                              <1> ;     folder that contains no files anywhere in its subtree produces
    42                              <1> ;     no entries and therefore doesn't come back on unpack). Matches
    43                              <1> ;     the behavior of most real zip tools when run without an
    44                              <1> ;     explicit "add empty dirs" flag.
    45                              <1> ;   - Timestamps are a fixed placeholder (1980-01-01, 00:00:00) rather
    46                              <1> ;     than the file's real modification time - the RTC driver behind
    47                              <1> ;     `date`/`time` was left unwired here to keep this change scoped
    48                              <1> ;     to the container format itself; a later pass could feed it in.
    49                              <1> ;   - No -force flag: `pack` refuses if `<folder>.zip` already exists
    50                              <1> ;     in the current directory, and `unpack` overwrites a same-named
    51                              <1> ;     FILE in place but refuses if a same-named FOLDER is in the way.
    52                              <1> ;   - Not tested in a real boot/QEMU environment - only nasm
